  "title": "The four Trump indictments were four different legal stories",
  "question": "What did the four criminal indictments against Donald J. Trump actually charge, how did the count structures differ, what later rulings changed them, and what was the procedural state of each case by October 1, 2026?",
  "bluf": "The famous 91-count total was a real peak snapshot, but only New York reached a verdict; the other three cases ended before a jury resolved the allegations.",
  "evidence_cutoff": "2026-10-01T14:00:00-04:00",
  "evidence_cutoff_note": "The four charging instruments, New York jury instructions and verdict, the Florida appointment dismissal, Supreme Court immunity and obstruction opinions, the federal election dismissal motion, Georgia quashal orders and the replacement prosecutor's nolle memorandum were reviewed. New York appellate orders through May 19, 2026 were checked; no merits appellate decision was located. Official DOJ summaries of 2025 FOIA rulings concerning Volume II were also checked. Some filed objects are preserved from public docket mirrors where PACER or a court download path prevented direct retrieval; the source ledger records custody and claim limits.",
  "use_note": "Use for the criminal charging and disposition record only. Do not combine civil judgments, impeachments or investigations that produced no indictment. Preserve the difference between allegations, adjudicated conviction, pretrial legal dismissal, prosecutor nolle and appellate finality.",
